{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#389","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#389","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#389\",\"parent_revision\":\"6ac2395bfe1eb2ec95075f05ebecc64f4de59e4902942024ea63dc04bad80036\",\"root_revision\":\"6ac2395bfe1eb2ec95075f05ebecc64f4de59e4902942024ea63dc04bad80036\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe backlog MCP server (10 tools, 382+ tests) is tightly coupled to GitHub Issues as its issue-tracking backend. Every operation \u2014 add, list, sync, close, resolve, groom \u2014 assumes GitHub Issues API. This prevents using the backlog system with GitLab, Jira, databases, or alternative agent task systems like GSD (get-shit-done), Superpowers, or Beads. Success: a clean backend protocol/interface with 2+ working backends shipped (GitHub Issues as default + at least one alternative like Beads graph tracker or SQLite) proving the abstraction works. You know it works when the same 10 MCP tools produce equivalent behavior against a different backend with zero changes to skill workflows or MCP tool signatures. Research refs: research/agent-frameworks/get-shit-done.md, research/agent-frameworks/superpowers.md, research/task-management/beads.md.\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nDate: 2026-04-01\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 PRIMARY coupling surface: all 10 MCP operations (add, list, view, groom, sync, close, resolve, update, normalize, pull) implemented here with direct GitHub Issues API calls; entire module must be restructured behind the `BacklogBackend` protocol | Risk: HIGH | Why: 271 GitHub-related references, every operation assumes GitHub; this is the refactoring target\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gh_client.py` \u2014 GitHub API client wrapper; becomes the GitHub backend implementation module | Risk: HIGH | Why: imported by operations.py, server.py, artifact_provider.py, github_sync.py; must be extracted into a `backends/github/` namespace\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/github_sync.py` \u2014 GitHub sync logic (pull/push between local YAML and GitHub Issues); becomes part of the GitHub backend | Risk: HIGH | Why: imported by operations.py, artifact_provider.py, server.py; sync behavior must be modelled in the `BacklogBackend` protocol\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/github_branches.py` \u2014 GitHub branch list/create operations; GitHub-specific; must move to GitHub backend | Risk: HIGH | Why: imported by operations.py and server.py; branch operations may not be part of the generic protocol \u2014 scope decision required\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` \u2014 40+ `@mcp.tool()` definitions; AC requires zero signature changes; currently delegates directly to operations.py; must be updated to call through a backend factory | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: tool signatures preserved, but call-site wiring changes; any change to how backend context is injected affects every tool\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/artifact_provider.py` \u2014 imports gh_client and github_sync; provides artifact storage via GitHub; must be adapted to backend-agnostic artifact interface | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: directly imports GitHub-specific modules that will be reorganized\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py` \u2014 BacklogItem and related data models; must remain backend-agnostic; may need protocol type definitions added | Risk: LOW | Why: models are already data-only but the `BacklogBackend` protocol return types must be defined here or in a new `protocol.py`\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/dispatch_state.py` \u2014 imports from backlog_core; dispatch state tracks execution context; if operations.py interface changes, any direct call site must be updated | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: direct import of backlog_core; coupling to operations API that is being restructured\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/artifact_registry.py` \u2014 imports from backlog_core; manages artifact registration; coupled to current operations/sync pattern | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: imports backlog_core internals that will be reorganized under backend namespaces\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/server.py` \u2014 imports from backlog_core; SAM sub-system exposes its own MCP server that calls into backlog_core | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: depends on operations.py API; must be verified compatible after backend refactor\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/cli.py` \u2014 imports operations directly; SAM CLI calls backlog_core operations | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: direct import of operations module being restructured\\n- `plugins/development-harness/scripts/run_backlog_server.py` \u2014 launches the backlog MCP server; will need backend configuration passed at startup | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: if the backend factory requires env var or config file at launch, server startup must pass it\\n- `plugins/development-harness/scripts/verify_migration_fidelity.py` \u2014 imports operations; verifies migration correctness; may test GitHub-specific behavior that must be separated from protocol-level behavior | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: imports operations.py directly; will need to target GitHub backend explicitly after refactor\\n- `plugins/development-harness/scripts/migrate_backlog_to_yaml.py` \u2014 imports from backlog_core; migration script | Risk: LOW | Why: likely uses parsing/yaml_io which are backend-agnostic; verify imports after refactor\\n- `plugins/development-harness/scripts/migrate_plan_artifacts.py` \u2014 imports from backlog_core | Risk: LOW | Why: artifact migration; may use artifact_provider which is being restructured\\n- `plugins/development-harness/scripts/dh_migrate.py` \u2014 imports from backlog_core | Risk: LOW | Why: general migration script; verify import compatibility after restructure\\n- `plugins/python3-development/scripts/migrate_tasks_to_github.py` \u2014 **CROSS-PLUGIN**: imports from backlog_core in development-harness from a different plugin | Risk: HIGH | Why: cross-plugin dependency on a module being fully restructured; no test coverage found in local test suite; breaks silently if import paths change\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/scripts/task_status_hook.py` \u2014 imports from backlog_core; hook script used in implementation manager | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: hooks run at agent lifecycle events; import failure would silently break the hook\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/scripts/implementation_manager.py` \u2014 imports from backlog_core | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: implementation manager orchestration depends on backlog_core APIs\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/__init__.py` \u2014 public API surface of the module; re-exports determine what consumers can import; must be updated when backend modules are reorganized | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: every consumer uses paths defined in __init__; reorganizing backends changes the re-export map\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/yaml_io.py` \u2014 YAML read/write for local backlog cache; backend-agnostic by nature; should remain unchanged | Risk: LOW | Why: file I/O layer, not GitHub-coupled; verify it is not called with GitHub-specific assumptions\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py` \u2014 markdown/frontmatter parsing; backend-agnostic | Risk: LOW | Why: parser has no GitHub dependency; no change expected\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/entry_blocks.py` \u2014 timestamped entry block management; backend-agnostic | Risk: LOW | Why: entry block format is independent of backend\\n\\n### Tests\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_github.py` \u2014 tests GitHub-specific client and wrapper logic; becomes GitHub backend unit tests; must be reorganized | Risk: HIGH | Why: tests GitHub-coupling that will be extracted to a backend module; test path and imports will change\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_github_sync.py` \u2014 tests github_sync module; becomes GitHub backend sync tests | Risk: HIGH | Why: directly tests a module that will move to backends/github/\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_github_branches.py` \u2014 tests GitHub branch operations | Risk: HIGH | Why: tests GitHub-specific behavior that will move to the GitHub backend\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_issues_graphql.py` \u2014 tests GraphQL sync path | Risk: HIGH | Why: GraphQL is GitHub-specific; must be moved to GitHub backend test suite\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_graphql_helpers.py` \u2014 tests GraphQL helper utilities | Risk: HIGH | Why: entirely GitHub-specific; moves to GitHub backend tests\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/graphql_factories.py` \u2014 GraphQL test factories; GitHub-specific fixtures | Risk: HIGH | Why: factory data is GitHub-shaped; must stay co-located with GitHub backend tests\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_operations.py` \u2014 tests operations.py; currently tests GitHub-coupled implementations | Risk: HIGH | Why: after refactor, must test via BacklogBackend protocol; fixtures likely instantiate GitHub client directly\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_server.py` \u2014 tests all 10+ MCP tool definitions; must continue to pass with zero signature changes | Risk: HIGH | Why: AC verification test; if any tool signature changes, this file proves the breakage\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backend_status.py` \u2014 already tests backend status concept; may represent prior partial work on abstraction | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: may already model the BacklogBackend protocol \u2014 read before designing to avoid duplication\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_scenarios.py` \u2014 integration scenario tests; tests real workflow flows | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: scenarios test tool-to-operation-to-github path; must be verified against both GitHub and new backends\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_operations_sam.py` \u2014 tests SAM-specific operations path | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: tests operations.py with SAM context; must be retargeted after operations.py is refactored\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/conftest.py` \u2014 shared fixtures; likely provisions GitHub client mocks | Risk: HIGH | Why: all test files inherit from conftest; if fixtures instantiate GitHub-specific objects directly, they block backend-agnostic testing\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_comment_operations.py` \u2014 tests comment operations; imports gh_client and github_sync | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: comment operations may be GitHub-specific; scope decision needed on whether comments are in the BacklogBackend protocol\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_backlog_config.py` \u2014 tests backlog configuration; likely tests GitHub config | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: configuration test must cover backend factory config (env var / config file)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_groom_mark_groomed.py` \u2014 tests groom-mark-groomed operation | Risk: LOW | Why: groomed marking is likely protocol-level behavior\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests_backlog/test_artifact_registry.py` \u2014 tests artifact registry | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: artifact registry imports backlog_core internals being reorganized\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests_backlog/test_artifact_provider.py` \u2014 tests artifact_provider which imports gh_client | Risk: HIGH | Why: artifact_provider is being restructured with gh_client extraction\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests_backlog/test_backlog_update_artifact_registration.py` \u2014 tests artifact registration on update | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: update operation is in operations.py which will be refactored\\n- `tests/test_implementation_manager/test_github_flag.py` \u2014 tests GitHub flag in implementation manager; explicitly tests GitHub behavior | Risk: HIGH | Why: tests GitHub-specific flag that may become a backend-selection mechanism\\n\\n### Documentation\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` \u2014 documents current architecture with GitHub Issues as the sole backend; entire document becomes stale | Risk: HIGH | Why: must be rewritten to describe BacklogBackend protocol, backend registry, and factory pattern\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/DOCUMENTATION_DRIFT_AUDIT.md` \u2014 existing drift audit; documents drift between docs and code | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: will accumulate new drift as backend restructure proceeds; should be re-run post-implementation\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/README.md` \u2014 user-facing backlog README; references GitHub Issues as the backend | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: must be updated to describe backend configuration and available backends\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/CLI_TO_MCP_MIGRATION.md` \u2014 migration guide from CLI to MCP; assumes GitHub backend | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: migration guide will reference backend-specific config that changes\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/github-integration.md` \u2014 explicitly describes GitHub integration behavior | Risk: HIGH | Why: this document describes GitHub-specific behavior that will be partitioned into backend; must be updated to explain backend abstraction\\n- `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-03-10-backlog-mcp-timestamped-entry-blocks.md` \u2014 plan doc referencing backlog MCP architecture | Risk: LOW | Why: plan/historical doc; low risk of causing confusion\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n- `.github/workflows/backlog-sync.yml` \u2014 GitHub Actions workflow that calls `backlog_backlog_sync` via `uv run -q fastmcp call plugins/development-harness/.mcp.json backlog_backlog_sync`; commits results to `.claude/backlog/` | Risk: HIGH | Why: this workflow drives the real GitHub Issues sync; if the sync operation requires a backend selection env var, the workflow must set it; if the GitHub backend module path changes, the `.mcp.json` server config may break\\n- `plugins/development-harness/.mcp.json` \u2014 MCP server configuration (referenced in backlog-sync.yml); must correctly point to the server.py entry point after restructure | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: if server.py moves or gains a backend factory init, `.mcp.json` may need updated env vars or entry point; file not found in current directory scan (may be generated at runtime or path differs)\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/groom-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 uses backlog_groom, backlog_view, backlog_update; explicitly named in AC as must-not-change | Risk: LOW | Why: AC says zero changes; skill references tool names only, not backend; LOW unless GitHub-specific behavior is embedded in instructions\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 orchestrates full workflow using backlog_list, backlog_view, backlog_update, backlog_resolve, backlog_close; primary user-facing workflow | Risk: LOW | Why: references MCP tool names only; backend abstraction is transparent to skill layer\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/step-procedures.md` \u2014 detailed step-by-step procedures calling every MCP tool | Risk: LOW | Why: tool names preserved; procedures remain valid\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/github-integration.md` \u2014 explicitly describes GitHub Issues behavior (labels, issue numbers, PR linking) | Risk: HIGH | Why: this reference document encodes GitHub-specific behavior as if it is universal backlog behavior; must be scoped to \\\"when using Git